  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "When estimating a vector of parameters, Equation A4.6 can be generalized by using the Hessian matrix, H, the matrix of second partials of the log-likelihood, whose ijth element is given by",
      "source_page": 867,
      "printed_page": 847,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p867:b14"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        54.0,
        1440.0,
        1011.0,
        1537.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_appendix1_formula205]]",
      "source_page": 868,
      "printed_page": 848,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p868:b2"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        516.0,
        161.0,
        736.0,
        232.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "H( $ \\Theta_{o} $) refers to the Hessian matrix evaluated at the point $ \\Theta_{o} $ and provides a measure of the local curvature of L around that point. The Fisher information matrix (F), the negative of expected value of the Hessian matrix for L,",
      "source_page": 868,
      "printed_page": 848,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p868:b4"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        148.0,
        237.0,
        1108.0,
        336.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_appendix1_formula206]]",
      "source_page": 868,
      "printed_page": 848,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p868:b5"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        503.0,
        343.0,
        752.0,
        379.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "provides a measure of the multidimensional curvature of the log-likelihood surface. Alternately, F can be computed as the expected value of the outer product of the score vector,",
      "source_page": 868,
      "printed_page": 848,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p868:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        149.0,
        387.0,
        1107.0,
        480.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_appendix1_formula207]]",
      "source_page": 868,
      "printed_page": 848,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p868:b8"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        474.0,
        479.0,
        779.0,
        517.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The covariance matrix for the MLEs is simply the inverse of the information matrix, with",
      "source_page": 868,
      "printed_page": 848,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p868:b10"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        149.0,
        523.0,
        1107.0,
        585.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_appendix1_formula208]]",
      "source_page": 868,
      "printed_page": 848,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p868:b11"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        472.0,
        584.0,
        782.0,
        634.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "As in the univariate case, if the likelihood surface is highly curved (very peaked) around the MLE, then the standard errors (being the inverse of the local curvature) are small, while if the likelihood is very flat, the sampling variance is large. For large sample sizes, F is often approximated by the Hessian matrix evaluated at the MLE,",
      "source_page": 868,
      "printed_page": 848,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p868:b13"
      ],
      "bbox": [
